include includes/folio-shared.pug

html(lang="en")
    head
        +folioHead('Curbside Pickup: what happened after check-in? | Aaron Benjamin', 'I designed Home Depot pickup messaging that let customers share an ETA, confirm check-in, and know what to expect next.', '/curbside-pickup.html', 'folio', '/assets/img/folio/project--curbside-pickup/curbside-pickup--checkin-complete-2x.png')
    body.folio.folio--case
        +folioShell
        +folioHeader('work', 'Curbside Pickup', '/#curbside-pickup')
        main#main.folio-main.folio-main--case
            article.folio-case
                header.folio-case__hero
                    figure.folio-case__hero-media.folio-case__hero-media--phone
                        img(src="/assets/img/folio/project--curbside-pickup/curbside-pickup--email-complete-2x.png" alt="Mobile ready-for-pickup email with arrival-time choices and an I’m here button" width="900" height="1940" fetchpriority="high")
                    .folio-case__intro
                        p.folio-eyebrow The Home Depot · Curbside Pickup
                        h1 The wait was short. The uncertainty was not.
                        p.folio-case__dek In early 2020, contactless pickup had to work before every system caught up. I put arrival timing and check-in confirmation into the messages customers already received.
                    +caseFacts
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Role
                            dd Staff UX Designer
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Scope
                            dd Field research, pickup email, and check-in messaging
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Key decision
                            dd Make arrival timing a two-way signal between customer and store
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Outcome
                            dd A 50-store, 60-day pilot reported clearer expectations and fewer calls to switch fulfillment

                +caseSection({labelledby: "curb-field-title"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow I — Inspiration
                    h2#curb-field-title Customers could arrive without knowing what happened next
                    p A customer could arrive, check in, and still wonder whether the store knew they were there. An average delivery time could describe operations without explaining that uncertainty. The problem was also about what happened between an arrival signal and a visible response.
                    p Home Depot was building contactless pickup while operations and customer habits changed week to week. Associates, signage, and email carried much of the experience. Missing check-in feedback left customers unsure whether the store knew they were there.
                    p I interviewed store managers, associates, and customers. Order complexity and store layout affected delivery time. Fulfillment changes disrupted the floor. Customers expected confirmation after check-in, but did not share one definition of “on time.”
                    p Store-operations analysis and surveys gave us a starting point: customers expected delivery in under five minutes, average door-to-door time was 3.5 minutes, and 30% of pickup customers called to change their pickup method.

                +imageBrief({"role": "Context", "title": "Two sides of the same wait", "composition": "Draw customer arrival and store preparation as parallel lanes with a missing check-in acknowledgement between them. No invented journey timings.", "purpose": "Establish the opening tension.", "caption": "Reconstructed feedback gap between customer and store.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "curb-align-title", tint: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow D — Design Process
                        h2#curb-align-title The email became a way to share arrival timing
                        p I framed the problem around missing feedback. Customers needed to know whether check-in worked and what to expect while waiting. Stores needed an arrival signal to help prioritize orders.
                        p I designed those signals into the ready-for-pickup email and check-in confirmation. The flow needed explicit help and delay states, since successful check-in was not the only outcome.
                    +storyFlow({id: "curb-flow-caption", title: "Arrival and pickup feedback", nodes: [{label: "Ready", detail: "Tell the customer when and where to collect."}, {label: "ETA", detail: "Let the customer share their arrival time."}, {label: "Check in", detail: "Confirm that the store received the signal."}, {label: "Wait or get help", detail: "Explain a delay or provide a call-store path."}], caption: "Arrival timing supports store preparation; confirmation reduces uncertainty for the customer."})


                +imageBrief({"role": "Process", "title": "The decision and its alternatives", "composition": "Draw ready → ETA → check-in → acknowledgement, with delay and call-store branches. Show customer and store signals distinctly.", "purpose": "Make the research-to-decision connection visible.", "caption": "A reconstructed comparison of the choices described in this story.", "ratio": "3:2", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "curb-engagement"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow E — Engagement
                    h2#curb-engagement Connect the customer’s wait to the store’s work
                    p Field interviews connected three perspectives: customers waiting for an order, associates preparing it, and managers coordinating the floor. A store’s layout and an order’s complexity affected the same promise customers were trying to interpret. The design needed to give both sides useful information without pretending every pickup was identical.
                    p I used the missing-feedback problem to explain why expectation-setting belonged in the messages customers already received. An ETA could help the store prepare; a confirmation could tell the customer the signal had arrived. The point was a two-way exchange that supported the operation, rather than another instruction customers had to remember.
                    p The constraint was a changing service with systems still catching up. I designed the pickup email and check-in messaging within that environment, keeping help and delay paths explicit. A successful check-in was only one state; an error or delay needed a useful response too.
                +caseSection({labelledby: "curb-eta-title", prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow A — Action
                        h2#curb-eta-title Check-in confirmation had to cover delays and errors too
                        p The email explained when and where to collect, asked for an ETA, provided help and Spanish-language access, and avoided unrelated fulfillment options.
                        p Check-in messaging covered success, errors that required a call to the store, and an associate-reported delay. Each state gave the customer an expectation or a recovery path.
                    +caseFigure({class: "folio-case__figure--portrait", labelledby: "curb-email-caption", src: "/assets/img/folio/project--curbside-pickup/curbside-pickup--email-complete-2x.png", alt: "Ready-for-pickup message with ETA choices of 10, 15, 20, and 30 minutes and an I’m here button", width: "900", height: "1940", loading: "lazy", caption: "The pickup email combines timing, arrival choices, and a check-in action.", captionId: "curb-email-caption"})
                    +caseFigure({labelledby: "curb-checkin-caption", src: "/assets/img/folio/project--curbside-pickup/curbside-pickup--checkin-complete-2x.png", alt: "Check-in confirmation messages for success, a call-store error, and a delay", width: "1140", height: "1140", loading: "lazy", caption: "Success, error, and delay messages each explain what happens next.", captionId: "curb-checkin-caption"})

                +caseSection({labelledby: "curb-delivery-detail"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow A — Action
                    h2#curb-delivery-detail Confirmation was part of the service
                    p The ready-for-pickup email was already a point of contact customers used. I brought timing and arrival choices into that message, alongside collection instructions, help, and Spanish-language access. Removing unrelated fulfillment choices kept attention on the pickup task.
                    p The confirmation then had to answer the next question: what should I expect now? Success, a call-store error, and an associate-reported delay were different situations. Each needed an expectation or a recovery path. Designing those states together kept the experience from ending at the check-in button.
                    p The reported pilot results should be read with the operational context intact. A 3.8-minute average with an ETA and a 4.5-minute average without one were both under the five-minute goal. They do not isolate the effect of my messaging from store preparation or other changes. The more useful result was the combination of reported expectations and fewer calls to switch fulfillment.
                    p This was the same problem in two directions: the store needed an arrival signal, and the customer needed feedback. The message connected those needs without claiming to remove every source of delay.

                +caseOutcome("curb-outcome-title", "Clearer expectations in the store pilot")
                    +caseResults([{value: "≈90%", label: "Reported that pickup arrived when expected"}, {value: "3.8 min", label: "Average door-to-door time with an ETA"}, {value: "<10%", label: "Called to switch fulfillment, versus a prior baseline above 30%"}], "Source: project presentation, 60-day pilot in 50 stores. Retrospective results have not been independently revalidated.")
                    p The presentation reports 4.5 minutes without ETAs. Both averages were under the five-minute goal; the figures do not isolate the effect of messaging from operational changes.
                    p My takeaway: an arrival signal helps the store prepare, and a confirmation helps the customer wait. Both sides need feedback when the process changes.
                +imageBrief({"role": "Outcome", "title": "What the evidence supports", "composition": "Use only the results stated in this case. Keep population, timeframe, qualitative status, and any attribution limits beside the result.", "purpose": "Resolve the story without overstating impact.", "caption": "Evidence summary reconstructed from the documented account; no invented measurements.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Evidence summary"})
            +folioFooter(false, 'I’m happy to discuss the field research and pickup communication flow.', {href: '/price-adjustments.html', label: 'Next: Price Adjustments'})
